ES_WITH", "targetSlug": "treaty-of-kars", "targetName": "Treaty of Kars", "context": "Georgia participated in the Treaty of Kars negotiations, impacting its border with Turkey."}], "places": [{"name": "Kars, Turkey", "role": "Signing location"}, {"name": "Yerevan, Armenia", "role": "Capital city"}, {"name": "Tbilisi, Georgia", "role": "Capital city"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Treaty of Kars", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "treaty-of-kars"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q935881", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Treaty_of_Kars"], "tags": ["contract", "International Treaties & Conventions", "cause-and-effect"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "Treaty of Kars contributed to the historical record of the Classical period. Key lasting impacts include: Shaped diplomatic relations and international order. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "The Treaty of Kars significantly shaped the geopolitical landscape of the South Caucasus by establishing borders that still influence regional relations today, particularly between Armenia and Azerbaijan. It played a crucial role in defining Turkey's territorial integrity in the post-Ottoman era.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-07-27T05:51:33Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#30240806025", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "The Treaty of Kars was a treaty that established the borders between Turkey and the three Transcaucasian Soviet republics, which are now the independent republics of Armenia, Georgia and Azerbaijan. The treaty was signed in the city of Kars on 13 October 1921.", "newValue": "The Treaty of Kars, signed on October 13, 1921, in Kars, Turkey, was a pivotal agreement that delineated the borders between the Republic of Turkey and the Soviet republics of Armenia, Georgia, and Azerbaijan. This treaty not only resolved territorial disputes stemming from the aftermath of World War I but also reinforced Turkey's sovereignty in the region, following the collapse of the Ottoman Empire. Key figures involved in the negotiations included Turkish Foreign Minister Yusuf Kemal Bey and Soviet leaders such as Leon Trotsky, highlighting the interplay between national interests and revolutionary ideologies in the early 20th century.

